# Line endings, and why this file is load-bearing rather than housekeeping.
#
# This repository pins bytes. `S-G1 · Der Keim` compares a generator export against a recorded
# sha256; the UVTT fixture asserts its own digest; the `.chronicle` round-trip gate requires
# export -> import -> empty diff; and gate `G-RL1 · Nachgerechnet` demands byte-identical replay
# on Windows, macOS and Linux. Every one of those is a statement about BYTES.
#
# With `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default, and what this machine has) and no rules here,
# Git rewrites LF to CRLF **on checkout**. The committed blobs stay clean — verified: the text
# fixtures carry 0 CR bytes in the object database — but a fresh clone gets different bytes on
# disk than the ones the tests were written against. Measured by session -22 in a clean
# worktree: `sampleMap.dd2vtt` gains 379 CR bytes (3,786,608 -> 3,786,987) and its sha256 flips,
# so `uvtt.test.ts` fails on a machine where nothing is wrong except the checkout.
#
# Because the blobs are already LF, this file is sufficient on its own. `git add --renormalize`
# is NOT required — that would only be needed if CRLF had been baked into the objects.
#
# The failure mode this prevents is the nastiest kind: it never reproduces for the person who
# committed the fixture, only for the next person to clone.

# Default: normalise text in the object database, and check it out with LF everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Byte-pinned fixtures — never transformed in either direction.
design/fixtures/** -text
packages/forge/test/fixtures/** -text
packages/io/test/fixtures/** -text
packages/io/schema/** -text
*.chronicle -text
*.dd2vtt -text
*.df2vtt -text
*.uvtt -text
*.dungeondraft_map -text

# Binary formats. Git's NUL-byte heuristic already catches most of these; saying it explicitly
# means a future format with a sparse header cannot slip through.
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ico binary

# Lockfiles and generated output are diffed by humans rarely and by tools often; keep them LF.
package-lock.json text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
